Hono server. Routes are registered in `src/index.ts`. Handlers stay thin; shared logic goes in `src/lib/`.

{{#if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}
The app is a Worker. `dev` runs `wrangler dev` in the Workers runtime, so the bindings declared in `wrangler.jsonc` are on `c.env` and the app listens on port {{#if (isMono)}}{{appPort appName}}{{else}}3000{{/if}}{{#unless (isMono)}} (`dev.port` in `wrangler.jsonc`){{/unless}}. There is no `start` script and no `src/server.ts`: nothing serves the app from Node or Bun.{{#if (has "tooling" "portless")}} Portless strips `--port` and hands the port down through `PORT`, which Wrangler does not read: under portless the Worker listens on Wrangler's default port ({{#unless (isMono)}}the `dev.port` of `wrangler.jsonc`{{else}}8787{{/unless}}) and the `.localhost` URL does not reach it.{{/if}}
{{else}}
{{#if (eq pm "bun")}}`dev` and `start` run under Bun{{else}}`dev` and `start` run on Node through `tsx`, from `src/server.ts` (it serves the app of `src/index.ts` with `@hono/node-server`){{/if}} and listen on port {{#if (isMono)}}{{appPort appName}}{{else}}3000{{/if}}. The `PORT` environment variable overrides it.
{{/if}}

The error handler in `src/app.ts` keeps the status and message of an `HTTPException` and answers any other error with a generic 500; the real error is always logged with its stack. It returns the message and stack only when `NODE_ENV=development`{{#if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}, which is the case under `{{runScript "dev"}}` and `{{runAppScript "preview"}}` (Wrangler builds the Worker for development) and not once deployed{{else}}, so run `NODE_ENV=development {{runScript "dev"}}` to see them in responses{{/if}}. Throw `HTTPException` for errors meant for the client.
